<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melk is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, at the western end of the Wachau valley. Its castle was an early residence of the Babenberg margraves, who gave it to Benedictine monks in 1089. The present Melk Abbey, rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style by Jakob Prandtauer between 1702 and 1736, is one of the best-known monasteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melk.jpg" alt="Melk" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C.Stadler/Bwag · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melk_-_Stift_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Melk lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> next to the Wachau valley on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The town is first mentioned as Medilica in 831 in a donation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, its name coming from a Slavic word for border. In 976 the area was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-margrave-of-austria">Leopold I, Margrave of Austria</a>, to serve as a buffer between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> to the west. A mention in 996 of an area known as Ostarrîchi is the origin of the German word Österreich.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The bluff that carries the abbey held a Babenberg castle until Leopold II gave the site to Benedictine monks from nearby Lambach in 1089. Melk received market rights in 1227 and became a municipality in 1898. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> reached 15.8 metres above its usual level in 1501, and the Schiffsmeisterhaus bears marks of high water on its facade. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> a subcamp of Mauthausen concentration camp operated at Melk. Its former crematorium is now a museum, and the rest of the camp became part of the Birago barracks of the Austrian Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Melk is best known for its massive baroque Benedictine monastery, Melk Abbey, but the town itself holds architecture of many centuries in a small area. The Rathaus on the Rathausplatz was built in 1575, and the Kolomanbrunnen fountain in the square is a gift from the abbey. The Lebzelterhaus dates from 1657, and the Haus am Stein, built in the 15th century, is the oldest building in town. The Old Post Office was built in 1792. The Gothic parish church holds a Kalvarienberg sculpture group, and the Wiener Tor stood until 1874. About five kilometres to the south stands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castle Schallaburg. Melk appears in fiction as the home of Adso, the co-protagonist of Umberto Eco's The Name of the Rose.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…architecture of Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach and Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt, the Karlskirche, the Belvedere and the abbey of <b>Melk</b> defined a Central European style, and in the later eighteenth century Christoph Willibald Gluck, Joseph Haydn and Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart created the Viennese Classical style in music.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their seat moved from <b>Melk</b> to Tulln, Klosterneuburg and finally Vienna around 1145.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-austria">Margraviate of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
